=encoding utf8

=head1 NAME

SDL3::API::Pixels - functions and constants declared in SDL_pixels.h

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  use SDL3::API::Pixels ':all';

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Low-level binding generated from the C header. Functions keep their C
names and argument order. Pointers to structs are objects, out parameters
take scalar references, C<void *> becomes a L<SDL3::Pointer>.

=head1 FUNCTIONS

=head2 SDL_GetPixelFormatName

  my $result = SDL_GetPixelFormatName($format);

C: C<< const char * SDL_GetPixelFormatName(SDL_PixelFormat format) >>

Get the human readable name of a pixel format.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item format - integer. the pixel format to query.

=back

Returns string: the human readable name of the specified pixel format or "SDL_PIXELFORMAT_UNKNOWN" if the format isn't recognized.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetMasksForPixelFormat

  my $result = SDL_GetMasksForPixelFormat($format, $bpp, $Rmask, $Gmask, $Bmask, $Amask);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_GetMasksForPixelFormat(SDL_PixelFormat format, int * bpp, Uint32 * Rmask, Uint32 * Gmask, Uint32 * Bmask, Uint32 * Amask) >>

Convert one of the enumerated pixel formats to a bpp value and RGBA masks.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item format - integer. one of the SDL_PixelFormat values.

=item bpp - reference to a scalar holding integer, pointer object or undef. a bits per pixel value; usually 15, 16, or 32.

=item Rmask - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the red mask for the format.

=item Gmask - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the green mask for the format.

=item Bmask - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the blue mask for the format.

=item Amask - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the alpha mask for the format.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetPixelFormatForMasks

  my $result = SDL_GetPixelFormatForMasks($bpp, $Rmask, $Gmask, $Bmask, $Amask);

C: C<< SDL_PixelFormat SDL_GetPixelFormatForMasks(int bpp, Uint32 Rmask, Uint32 Gmask, Uint32 Bmask, Uint32 Amask) >>

Convert a bpp value and RGBA masks to an enumerated pixel format.

This will return `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_UNKNOWN` if the conversion wasn't possible.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item bpp - integer. a bits per pixel value; usually 15, 16, or 32.

=item Rmask - unsigned integer. the red mask for the format.

=item Gmask - unsigned integer. the green mask for the format.

=item Bmask - unsigned integer. the blue mask for the format.

=item Amask - unsigned integer. the alpha mask for the format.

=back

Returns integer: the SDL_PixelFormat value corresponding to the format masks, or SDL_PIXELFORMAT_UNKNOWN if there isn't a match.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetPixelFormatDetails

  my $result = SDL_GetPixelFormatDetails($format);

C: C<< const SDL_PixelFormatDetails * SDL_GetPixelFormatDetails(SDL_PixelFormat format) >>

Create an SDL_PixelFormatDetails structure corresponding to a pixel format.

Returned structure may come from a shared global cache (i.e. not newly allocated), and hence should not be modified, especially the palette. Weird errors such as `Blit combination not supported` may occur.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item format - integer. one of the SDL_PixelFormat values.

=back

Returns SDL3::PixelFormatDetails object or undef: a pointer to a SDL_PixelFormatDetails structure or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_CreatePalette

  my $result = SDL_CreatePalette($ncolors);

C: C<< SDL_Palette * SDL_CreatePalette(int ncolors) >>

Create a palette structure with the specified number of color entries.

The palette entries are initialized to white.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item ncolors - integer. represents the number of color entries in the color palette.

=back

Returns SDL3::Palette object or undef: a new SDL_Palette structure on success or NULL on failure (e.g. if there wasn't enough memory); call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_SetPaletteColors

  my $result = SDL_SetPaletteColors($palette, $colors, $firstcolor, $ncolors);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_SetPaletteColors(SDL_Palette * palette, const SDL_Color * colors, int firstcolor, int ncolors) >>

Set a range of colors in a palette.

It is safe to call this function from any thread, as long as the palette is not modified or destroyed in another thread.

=over

=item palette - SDL3::Palette object or undef. the SDL_Palette structure to modify.

=item colors - SDL3::Color object or undef. an array of SDL_Color structures to copy into the palette.

=item firstcolor - integer. the index of the first palette entry to modify.

=item ncolors - integer. the number of entries to modify.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_DestroyPalette

  SDL_DestroyPalette($palette);

C: C<< void SDL_DestroyPalette(SDL_Palette * palette) >>

Free a palette created with SDL_CreatePalette().

It is safe to call this function from any thread, as long as the palette is not modified or destroyed in another thread.

=over

=item palette - SDL3::Palette object or undef. the SDL_Palette structure to be freed.

=back

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_MapRGB

  my $result = SDL_MapRGB($format, $palette, $r, $g, $b);

C: C<< Uint32 SDL_MapRGB(const SDL_PixelFormatDetails * format, const SDL_Palette * palette, Uint8 r, Uint8 g, Uint8 b) >>

Map an RGB triple to an opaque pixel value for a given pixel format.

This function maps the RGB color value to the specified pixel format and returns the pixel value best approximating the given RGB color value for the given pixel format.

If the format has a palette (8-bit) the index of the closest matching color in the palette will be returned.

If the specified pixel format has an alpha component it will be returned as all 1 bits (fully opaque).

If the pixel format bpp (color depth) is less than 32-bpp then the unused upper bits of the return value can safely be ignored (e.g., with a 16-bpp format the return value can be assigned to a Uint16, and similarly a Uint8 for an 8-bpp format).

It is safe to call this function from any thread, as long as the palette is not modified.

=over

=item format - SDL3::PixelFormatDetails object or undef. a pointer to SDL_PixelFormatDetails describing the pixel format.

=item palette - SDL3::Palette object or undef. an optional palette for indexed formats, may be NULL.

=item r - unsigned integer. the red component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item g - unsigned integer. the green component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item b - unsigned integer. the blue component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=back

Returns unsigned integer: a pixel value.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_MapRGBA

  my $result = SDL_MapRGBA($format, $palette, $r, $g, $b, $a);

C: C<< Uint32 SDL_MapRGBA(const SDL_PixelFormatDetails * format, const SDL_Palette * palette, Uint8 r, Uint8 g, Uint8 b, Uint8 a) >>

Map an RGBA quadruple to a pixel value for a given pixel format.

This function maps the RGBA color value to the specified pixel format and returns the pixel value best approximating the given RGBA color value for the given pixel format.

If the specified pixel format has no alpha component the alpha value will be ignored (as it will be in formats with a palette).

If the format has a palette (8-bit) the index of the closest matching color in the palette will be returned.

If the pixel format bpp (color depth) is less than 32-bpp then the unused upper bits of the return value can safely be ignored (e.g., with a 16-bpp format the return value can be assigned to a Uint16, and similarly a Uint8 for an 8-bpp format).

It is safe to call this function from any thread, as long as the palette is not modified.

=over

=item format - SDL3::PixelFormatDetails object or undef. a pointer to SDL_PixelFormatDetails describing the pixel format.

=item palette - SDL3::Palette object or undef. an optional palette for indexed formats, may be NULL.

=item r - unsigned integer. the red component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item g - unsigned integer. the green component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item b - unsigned integer. the blue component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=item a - unsigned integer. the alpha component of the pixel in the range 0-255.

=back

Returns unsigned integer: a pixel value.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetRGB

  SDL_GetRGB($pixelvalue, $format, $palette, $r, $g, $b);

C: C<< void SDL_GetRGB(Uint32 pixelvalue, const SDL_PixelFormatDetails * format, const SDL_Palette * palette, Uint8 * r, Uint8 * g, Uint8 * b) >>

Get RGB values from a pixel in the specified format.

This function uses the entire 8-bit [0..255] range when converting color components from pixel formats with less than 8-bits per RGB component (e.g., a completely white pixel in 16-bit RGB565 format would return [0xff, 0xff, 0xff] not [0xf8, 0xfc, 0xf8]).

It is safe to call this function from any thread, as long as the palette is not modified.

=over

=item pixelvalue - unsigned integer. a pixel value.

=item format - SDL3::PixelFormatDetails object or undef. a pointer to SDL_PixelFormatDetails describing the pixel format.

=item palette - SDL3::Palette object or undef. an optional palette for indexed formats, may be NULL.

=item r - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the red component, may be NULL.

=item g - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the green component, may be NULL.

=item b - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the blue component, may be NULL.

=back

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetRGBA

  SDL_GetRGBA($pixelvalue, $format, $palette, $r, $g, $b, $a);

C: C<< void SDL_GetRGBA(Uint32 pixelvalue, const SDL_PixelFormatDetails * format, const SDL_Palette * palette, Uint8 * r, Uint8 * g, Uint8 * b, Uint8 * a) >>

Get RGBA values from a pixel in the specified format.

This function uses the entire 8-bit [0..255] range when converting color components from pixel formats with less than 8-bits per RGB component (e.g., a completely white pixel in 16-bit RGB565 format would return [0xff, 0xff, 0xff] not [0xf8, 0xfc, 0xf8]).

If the surface has no alpha component, the alpha will be returned as 0xff (100% opaque).

It is safe to call this function from any thread, as long as the palette is not modified.

=over

=item pixelvalue - unsigned integer. a pixel value.

=item format - SDL3::PixelFormatDetails object or undef. a pointer to SDL_PixelFormatDetails describing the pixel format.

=item palette - SDL3::Palette object or undef. an optional palette for indexed formats, may be NULL.

=item r - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the red component, may be NULL.

=item g - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the green component, may be NULL.

=item b - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the blue component, may be NULL.

=item a - reference to a scalar holding unsigned integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the alpha component, may be NULL.

=back

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.


